<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nghệ-Tĩnh Soviets</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a>–<a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Nghe-Tinh Soviets, Nghe-Tinh uprising</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nghệ-Tĩnh Soviets were a peasant uprising in the provinces of Nghệ An and Hà Tĩnh in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in 1930–1931, organised with the help of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a> during the Depression. Villagers set up local councils, seized land and stopped paying taxes. French forces crushed the movement with aerial bombing, executions and mass arrests, but it became a founding episode of Vietnamese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nghe-tinh-soviets.jpg" alt="Nghệ-Tĩnh Soviets" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arvn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nghe_Tinh_Soviet_Movement.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Nghệ An and Hà Tĩnh were among the poorest provinces inhabited by ethnic Vietnamese, with harvests that varied widely and too little land; about half the rural population was landless, as wealthy landlords took over communal lands from indebted farmers. In Nghệ An both the tenth-month <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> harvest of 1929 and the fifth-month harvest of 1930 failed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">Corvée labour</a>, the state monopolies on alcohol, salt and opium, and above all taxes were bitterly resented, as were corrupt mandarins and village notables. Communist groups had been building trade unions, peasant associations and women's and youth groups in both provinces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The unrest began in March 1930 with strikes in Vinh and Bến Thuỷ and spread to the countryside, where peasants demanded a halt to personal taxes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> and the return of communal lands. The first demonstrations the party organised were dispersed when the garde indigène fired on the crowds, killing 27 men, women and children. On 12 September aircraft dropped six bombs on a march at Hưng Nguyên, three kilometres from Vinh, killing between 140 and 200 people. Crowds then burned administrative buildings and railway stations, destroyed tax registers and attacked mandarins and notables.</p><p>Where administration collapsed, villagers met in the communal temple and elected committees, the village peasant sections that the party's leaders called soviets. Dominated by poorer peasants, they seized back communal lands, annulled local taxes, lowered rents, dug <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and opened schools, and they survived in west Nghệ An until August 1931 and in Hà Tĩnh until the end of that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>From January 1931 the French, with police from Tonkin, identity cards and a new pro-French governor of Nghệ An, Nguyen Khoa Ky, steadily regained control. During new demonstrations around May Day 1931 over 500 people were killed, and the outcry led to the dismissal of Le Fol, Resident-Superior of Annam, though his successor Yves Chatel proved harsher. The historian Bernal estimates that over 1300 people were killed and about a thousand died in prisons and camps, while 99 percent of the party's cadres were killed or arrested. Vietnamese historians stress the party's leadership; other scholars hold that much of the revolt was beyond its control.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nghệ-Tĩnh Soviets” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
